  "response": "A distemper-like disease was reported among Tasmanian carnivores in the early twentieth century. Researchers compiled bounty payments, museum accessions and sighting reports into a dated records database, and that compilation led Prowse and colleagues to publish a population-viability model of thylacine decline in 2013. Those early twentieth-century reports of sickness among the island's carnivores supplied the evidence on which the 2013 modelling study rested its conclusion that an introduced disease epidemic was the necessary primary driver of the extinction. The published assessment of the competing hypotheses determined that disease was not a necessary component of the extinction and identified bounty-driven hunting as the dominant cause, a determination that the population-viability modelling itself demonstrates; and that determination and the verdict that an epidemic was indispensable cannot both be true, so the attribution rests with the bounty campaign.\n\nSettlement also reshaped the island's interior, and at least one of two consequences followed there: thylacine habitat in the Tasmanian Midlands was converted to sheep pasture, or prey availability for thylacines declined across the settled districts of Tasmania, and perhaps both held at once. Either consequence would have narrowed the margin within which a large carnivore could persist in the pastoral belt.\n\nThe institutional record of persecution is unusually well documented. The Van Diemen's Land Company offered private bounties on thylacines from the 1830s, and only decades afterwards did the Tasmanian parliament introduce a government bounty, in 1888. Because that statutory scheme attached a standing cash value to every animal killed, it paid out 2,184 claims before it was discontinued in 1909. The Tasmanian bounty scheme paid out more than 15,000 claims, a total that cannot be squared with the 2,184 entered against the government scheme; the two tallies cannot both be true.\n\nGenomic sequencing of thylacine specimens revealed low genetic diversity predating European arrival. The sequencing drew on museum skins and skeletal material held in Australian and European collections, and the resulting portrait of the island population's genetic structure has become a fixed reference point in assessments of the species' resilience, since a narrow genetic base limits the variation available to any population confronting unfamiliar pressures.\n\nThe controversy over the thylacine's demise turned on whether deliberate persecution by settlers or pressures already acting on the species carried the greater weight, and resolving it required a single shared chronology rather than rival anecdotes. The thylacine was a marsupial carnivore endemic to Tasmania at the time of European colonisation, an insular restriction that followed from its disappearance from mainland Australia approximately 3,000 years ago. Any credible attribution therefore had to account for an animal that entered the colonial period as an island endemic, with no continental reservoir from which local losses could be replenished.",
